  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nNamed: FourierGreedy - Leverage spectral properties and adaptive grids for structured fluctuations.\nThe fundamental idea is to decompose the overlap problem into spectral components, exploit Chebyshev polynomial bases for bounded-range functions, and enforce energy constraints to penalize oscillations. This differs from the parent in directly optimizing Fourier coefficients under constraints, using exponential weightings to bias towards smoother solutions. The rationale is that optimal overlap configurations often exhibit near-periodic structure, reducible to low-frequency components. This prevents earlier, blind grid-scanning strategies that can easily get trapped in high-frequency chimeras.\n\nWe use a hierarchical optimization scheme with safeguards:\n1. Chebyshev expansions for bounded approximation\n2. Fourier-based spectral projection (log-energy domain)\n3. Progressively coarser grids for local exploitation\n4. Balanced frequency band annealing\n5. Energy flux constraints\n\nThe core mathematical formulation is to represent h(x) as a weighted combination of Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind (T_n(x/\u03c0 *\u221a3)), ensuring uniform boundedness on [0,2]. Coefficients are penalized via log-energy decay (flooring minimum energy in low bands). This regularizes the optimization while still allowing complex spatial structures.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: \u03c4_k coefficients for Chebyshev basis (k=0,...,N) with |\u03c4_k| < exp(-\u03b2k) (default \u03b2=2.5)\nTransformation: Fej\u00e9r-Riesz mapping to unit circle in complex plane (for phase).\nObjective: Minimize max_k C_k using Spectral Projected Gradient (SPG) solver\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent Initialization (n=256):\n   - Verify persistence of parent via upsampled Least Norm Projection (LNP)\n   - Convert to log-scale energy frame\n\n2. Chebyshev structure evolution:\n   - axial phase with Slerp(\u00b7,\u00b7,t) interpolation\n   - Multi-scale mode: start grid=32,256,1024\n   - For each grid (after parent):\n       a. Rebuild basis from parent using shifted T-reflection\n       b. PSO initialization (particles: coefficient vectors)\n       c. Adaptive spectral annealing (slide band-width)\n       d. SLSQP polish (using linear value transforms)\n\n3. Mode optimization (for grid points):\n   - Define T_k(x) polynomials (as initial basis)\n   - Express optimization variables: c_k = exp(-\u03c3 \u00b7 k) \u00b7 randn()\n   - Projection onto unit disk: \u2211 c_k^2 \u2264 budget\n   - Energy penalty: -\u03b2 log(\u039b_min) [soft]\n\n4. Spectral walk on band:\n   - Low band (k=0-32: 50% spectral weight)\n   - Medium band (k=33-64: 30%)\n   - High band (k=65+10%)\n   - Cooling schedule: T_max=500, to T_min=5 (geometric decay)\n\n5. Local refinement:\n   - Grid doubling with basis restart from parent\n   - Oscillation clamp: maximum modes kept from n to 2n\n   - Stabilizer: Kreiss-Pilant marker for constraint regret\n\n6. Objective evaluation:\n   - Use FFT-based convolution (irfft(prod FFTs))\n   - Cache intermediate buffering to avoid O(n^2)\n   - Clip normalization: integral = (1/dx) * sum(c_k^2)\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Representations: T-polynomial, exponential weighting, binary merge crossover.\n- Temperature schedules: plateau=7, drop factor=0.95.\n- Temperature evolution: linear cooling vs. log.\n- Three-phase optimization: build, refine, polish.\n\nComputation budget & robustness:\n- Assumed: 4 phases + 2 annealing cycles = 40% budget use.\n- Budget checkpoint: @500s monitor energy drops.\n- Full grid steps: up to 2048 points (budgeted 2s) [FFT scaling]\n- Spectral-based operations: O(n) per objective eval.\n\nObjective: minimize max_k (overlap at lag k) in complex domain.\n\nValidation:\n- Check integral: normalize exactly via LNP.\n- Check bounds: restrict c_k via \u221a(budget/\u03c3) caps.\n- Check FFT normalization: use ifftshift & fftn for periodic checks.\n- Return condition: strict energy drop in final SLSQP stage.",
